  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIt will be clear and cool this morning with areas of fog as the \n1028mb surface high sits overhead. Expect plenty of sunshine today, \nwith perhaps just a few high clouds streaming through late morning \nand early afternoon. Winds turn south and increase through the day, \nbecoming breezy by this evening at 8-15 mph with gusts to 20-25 mph \nover the Finger Lakes and surrounding locations. After the cool \nstart it should warm up nicely into the upper 60s to mid-70s this \nafternoon around the region.\n\nA decaying warm front tries to press in from the northwest, but runs \ninto the massive 594dm ridge over the area. Model guidance is \nhinting at some more cloud cover and perhaps a few light showers or \nsprinkles overnight into Wednesday morning, especially north of the \nNY Southern Tier. Most of Wednesday will be dry as well, but can't \nrule out a stray showers along the weak frontal boundary. There will \nbe more clouds north and more sun south through the day. Winds \nremain out of the south-southwest and decrease through the day, \neventually turning west-northwest late in the day along the I-90 \ncorridor. High temperatures range from the mid-70s to low 80s across \nthe area, and humidity levels increase by afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA weak low pressure system then moves through the area on Thursday. \nIt will remain warm and humid with dew points near 70 expected. \nThere will be modest amounts of instability, especially across the \nSouthern Tier and NE PA. PWATs will be anomalously high, between \n1.75 to 1.95 inches over the area, this is about 2.5 standard \ndeviations above normal for this time of year. This setup will bring \nthe potential for efficient rain producing showers and a few \nthunderstorms. There will be the potential for some heavy downpours \nand a low risk for excessive rainfall amounts. There does remain \nplenty of uncertainty on just how much instability will be present \non Thursday, so this will impact the potential for any organized \nconvection; which if it does materialize could bring gusty winds and \neven higher rainfall rates. The trends will need to be monitored \nclosely in the coming days. Otherwise, Thursday looks to be mainly \ncloudy, and very humid with highs in the mid-70s to low 80s again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure slides toward the region across southern Canada \nThursday night into Friday. Confidence appears to be increasing in \nthe latest model guidance that this will turn winds northerly for \nour area, and bring in a drier air mass. The NBM ensemble guidance \nnow has Friday completely dry, and mostly sunny with low humidity \nand seasonable high temperatures in the 70s. Most of Saturday also \nlooks dry in the latest ensemble data, but a late day warm front \ncould bring a low chance for showers over northern portions of \nCentral NY. Temperatures trend down a few degrees compared to Friday.\n\nThe latest guidance is also starting to come together on a more \nconsistent solution for the end of the upcoming weekend and early \nnext week. The models are showing a more active weather pattern as a \nseries of lows and waves move along a stalled frontal boundary along \nthe northern periphery of the Southeast ridge. This will keep more \nclouds and showers in the forecast, especially on Sunday...but could \nlinger into at least Monday. High pressure does try to move back in \nfrom the north by next Tuesday, but confidence is low this far out \nin time in the exact position of this feature. Temperatures look to \nbe seasonably cool in the time period, with low to mid 70s on Sunday \nfading into the 60s for highs by Monday/Tuesday. Overnight lows are \nexpected to be in the 50s Sunday night, then potentially falling \nback into the 40s Monday/Tuesday nights.",
